{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791444145,"nonce":"uQoxZS3FgslBcyCdmfKopcaMFg4=","message":"Isolated the phase with a minimal standalone Cranelift fixture using the pinned upstream bc2f967927f9f0e839095a752bf554c4a09fdd13 dependency, no Wasmtime fork changes.\n\nThe fixture takes two pointers, loads three f64 fields from each, adds each pair, calls an external allocator and stores the three results. Context::optimize itself changes the order from load/add before call to all six loads before call followed by three adds after it. This occurs before register allocation. egraph/mod.rs remove_pure_and_optimize removes pure fadd instructions from the side-effect skeleton; egraph/elaborate.rs elaborate_block materializes them at their first demanding stores after the call.\n\nARM64 native output reproduces six 16-byte spills/reloads and a 96-byte spill area. A diagnostic-only relocation of those exact three fixture additions before the call, after optimize and before TargetIsa::compile_function, reduces that to three spills/reloads and a 48-byte spill area. It does not invoke optimize a second time. The original x86-64 real-kernel dump shows the same six-operand spill pattern.\n\nReproducer /tmp/zyntax-clif-call-pressure.rs; input CLIF, optimized CLIF, ordinary native and explicitly placed native output /tmp/zyntax-clif-call-pressure.log locally. The temporary repo example was removed after capture. This proves the scheduling opportunity, not a runtime speedup or a safe general production pass. Generalizing requires proving operand availability across a call, accounting for other uses/register pressure, preserving trapping/memory order, and measuring short-run compile cost. No dependency pin or existing Wasmtime fork edits were changed.","files":null}]}